      "math_academy_id": "q-127946",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at $(1,-2,2)$ if $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2z-2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}z+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,2z-2xy,\\,2{x}^{2}z+2y⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-2,2) & =⟨2(1)(2)^{2}-(-2)^{2},\\,-2(1)(-2)+2(2),\\,2(1)^{2}(2)+2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-4,4+4,4-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,8,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
